I am a Computer Science undergraduate at Kansas State University,
where virtually all of the UNIX boxes available to me are SUN4
machines.  I'm in the market to buy a workstation, but have no idea
where to begin! :(

Not to start a holy war by asking "What is the best WorkStation", but
what is the best WorkStation?

Here is my wish-list.  Under the "Absolutely required features" are
the absolute minum requirements of the computer.  If the machine fails
any of these tests, I will not even consider it.  Then below I have an
additional wish-list with factors that would make the machine more
desireable to me.

		    Absolutely required features:
		    -----------------------------

Machine must run UNIX or UNIX deravitive.  The Operating System must
compare with the POSIX standard.

Machine processor must get at least 50 on the meaningless indicator of
processor speed.

Machine must have at least twelve megabytes of main memory and at
least four hundred megabytes of hard-drive space.

Machine must have at least one high-density 3.5" floppy drive.

Machine must include networking hardware and TCP/IP suite of network
protocols installed.

Machine must have high-resolution (at least 1024x768) color display.
Color means at least 8 bit video.  The monitor must be at least 15".

Machine must support any amount of primary or secondary memory,
increased video and/or sound capability, larger hard-drives, etc.

Vendor support over the Internet.

Industry standard peripheral support (e.g. modems, printers, monitors,
hard-drives, etc.)
				   
			 Additional Wishlist:
			 --------------------

Base system with more memory and/or hard-drive space than required.

CD-ROM, tape back-up with base unit.

Room for additional on-board hard-ware.

Pre-installed compilers / GNU utilities / elm / nn / UUCP / terminal
emulation.

Ability to increase processor speed at later date.

Graphics accelerator, greater than 8 bit graphics.

------ (end of list)

What should I be looking at?  Where should I look?  Who should I talk
to?

