I'm getting a strange problem with a SCSI disk on a MicroVAX 3100 10e.

The disk is a 750mb Fujitsu (M2263 I think), it's attached to 
the external SCSI port of the 3100. This disk is the only device
on the bus. There are no devices on SCSI-A of the system. 

The problem has been seen on VMS V5.4-2 and on A5.5-1.

The symptom is that occasionally while running BACKUP ( and only
when running BACKUP ) the disk will hang. The "drive being accessed"
light on the front of the disk is on steady. Rebooting the system or
power cycling the drive will clear the problem. No other errors are
observed on the disk.

This has happened on two different disks attached to two different
MV 3100s. I've replaced the drive, the system board and the SCSI
cable and terminator, none of these things have helped.

My guess is that the problem is a design problem in either the 3100
or the disk, but neither Digital nor Fujitsu will admit such
a problem exists. I know the SCSI chipset on the 3100 is not very
efficient, but I don't know if it causes problems like this.

Has anyone else encountered this problem, and if so is there any
known solution. I'd appreciate even knowing definitely which device
is at fault, so that I can figure out a way to reconfigure things to
work around the problem.

