In article <1992Nov20.215457.1595@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>, alan@nabeth.enet.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Alan's Home for Wayward Tumbleweeds.) writes:
...
|> >The /etc/disktab entry 'st42400n' has the following settings:
|> >	nc#2624:nt#19:ns#83
|> 
|> I don't know anything about the drive.  I'd call or contact the
|> vendor and ask them for their recommendations for using it on a
|> particular type of system.  The numbers look reasonable.  You
|> can verify what ULTRIX thinks about the geometry by using rzdisk.
|> One of the pages contains the geometry information.  If the geometry
|> is in the expected place, you can get ULTRIX to give you the information
|> needed by using the creatediskbyname() library function.

The ST42400N is a zoned drive, so it doesn't have a geometry which
can be expressed in disktab or made use of by the filing system.

The inner track has a capacity of 42000 bytes and the outer track has
a capacity of 55440 bytes (unformatted).  "ns" is clearly not a fixed
number.  I have the Seagate User's manual for this drive if anybody
is interested in anything else from it. 

Given that one must perforce invent a geometry to keep the filing
system happy, the numbers above are as good as any other arbitrary
numbers.

It's high time the filing system STOPPED TRYING to understand geometry
of SCSI discs, in my opinion.

-- 
