In article <1992Nov25.040842.11667@blaze.cs.jhu.edu>, ghouse@gauss.cs.jhu.edu (Mujtaba Ghouse) writes:
>
>Hi,
>
>One of my relatives is considering an upgrade from a VAX 6000 to a VAX 9000,
>and would like to know more about, say:

As will become clear from my answer, it is important to know WHAT
VAX 6000.  There are currently six different processors possible.

First, some general information.  The VAX 6000 is a family of systems
built around a common system enclosure and backplane.  Over the current
life of the family five different CPUs have been available not including
vector processors available with some models.  The system bus/backplane
is the XMI which supports as many 14 modules, up to 6 of which can be
CPUs, up to 8 can be memory and the remaining either adapters to VAXBI
buses or native XMI I/O devices.  Later family members use a version
of the XMI that supports a write back cache to memory and battery
backup.

The VAX 9000 comes in two configurations; the Model 200 and the Model
400.  In each configuration the CPU and memory are on a seperate
interconnect and the XMI is used exclusively as an I/O bus.  The
Model 200 is a single CPU system with an optional vector processor.
It uses a single XMI as the I/O bus.  The Model 400 supports up to
4 CPUs and four vector processors.  It can be configured with up to
four XMIs for I/O devices.  Both models support up to 512 MB of memory
(this may have increased since my information source was published).

There are also models available of the VAX 9000s that don't include
the "commercial" parts of the VAX instruction sets.

>      differences in speed

Using the speed of the VAX 11/780 (VUP) as the base, the relative (and
approximate speeds) of the VAX 6000 CPUs and VAX 9000 CPU are:

VAX 6000 Model 200 -  ~3 VUPs
VAX 6000 Model 300 -  ~3.8 VUPs
VAX 6000 Model 400 -  ~7 VUPs
VAX 6000 Model 500 - ~13 VUPs
VAX 6000 Model 600 - ~32 VUPs
VAX 9000           - ~40 VUPs

I believe all the VAX 6000 Models 300 to 600 can be configured
with up to six CPUs.  The VAX 6000 Model 200 may be limited to
four.  The Model 400 VAX 9000 systems can be have up to four.
The VAX 6000 Model 400 and 500 have optional vector processors
available.  I don't know if the vector CPU is also available
for the Model 600.

>
>      differences in configuration

The biggest difference in configuration potential is that VAX 6000
family uses a single XMI as the system bus where the VAX 9000 can
use up to four of them as I/O busses.  This allows configuring the
VAX 9000 with more I/O adapters and better distribution of them.

>
>      capabilities of the VAX 9000 (such as coarse grain parallelism ? )
>      that are not shared by the 6000.

I'm not sure there are any.  The VAX 6000 CPU family are all CMOS
implimenetations of the VAX CPU.  There are some instructions that
are emulated, which might not be in the VAX 9000.  Most of the VAX
6000 family can support two more CPUs than the VAX 9000.  Perhaps
the biggest difference in the two is that the VAX 9000 was designed
to compete in the mainframe market and offers reliability features
appropriate to that market.

