>
>> At NASA we run our realtime flight simulator on an SGI doing the
>> simulation models in one CPU and out the window visuals in another.
>> The entire frame runs in under 1ms,  I think you have experience
>> with the wrong machine running X.
>This example proves nothing.  The flight simulator is not an X program.
>If it was an X program there is no way you could generate frames in
>"under 1 ms".

Vendors who do fast X graphics (SGI, HP, etc.) use an extended X
driver that creates a transparent "hole" in the X server for special
graphics.  For example, the HP uses a 4 plane overlay for X on their
SRX series of graphics tops.  The remaining 24 planes are not
controlled by the X server.  When you make a graphics window on the
24 plane device using HP's Starbase graphics, a corresponding
transparent hole opens in the X server so you can see it.  In any
event, the graphics are displayed at DMA speed without intervention
by the X server.

By the by, the HP SRX blows the tails off the SGI 4D stuff, and the
SRX is now an antique!  What the HP does to a VAXStation is enough to
get the person responsible for purchase fired!

